  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kenya: Supporting Prison Reforms in Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) Prison officers have increased knowledge and tools of how to conduct correctional management, in line with international standards. 2) The State Department for Correctional Services has increased knowledge of how to develop prison policy and legal frameworks compliant with international standards on corrections. 3) The Kenyan Judiciary, the Office of the Director of Public Prosecutions (ODPP), the National Police Service (NPS), and the State Department for Correctional Services (SDCS) have increased capacity to apply prison decongestion measures, in line with existing policies, laws, strategies and guidelines.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Direct beneficiaries: The Ministry of Interior, and specifically the SDCS, Kenya Prison Service (KPS), Probation and Aftercare Services (PACs), prison officials serving in the targeted detention facilities, the Kenyan Judiciary, ODPP, NPS, and the National Council on Administration of Justice (NCAJ). Indirect beneficiaries: The prison population, especially in the prisons selected by the project.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to enable the Kenyan State Department of Correctional Services to deliver services in line with international standards on the treatment of prisoners.</narrative>
  </description>
